{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 8, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from the year 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by constant warfare with Muslim forces. His early life involved captivity after the Battle of Inab where he spent roughly ten years as a prisoner in Aleppo before gaining freedom through ransom payment. This long imprisonment shaped his worldview and made him deeply skeptical of reckless military adventures while fostering a pragmatic approach to diplomacy with neighboring Muslim leaders.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond assumed power following the assassination of his father Raymond II and he immediately faced threats from the Order of Assassins as well as internal noble factions seeking influence over the county. His time in captivity taught him the Arabic language and gave him unique insight into Islamic politics that few other Crusader nobles possessed. These skills allowed him to negotiate truces and maintain stability along the northern borders of the Latin states despite ongoing pressure from Nur ad-Din and later Saladin.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nHe acted as regent for Baldwin IV when the young king suffered from leprosy and could not rule independently due to his deteriorating health condition. Raymond sought to secure the succession through marriage alliances involving Sibylla yet he often clashed with the faction led by Guy of Lusignan who favored aggressive confrontation with Muslim armies. The count advocated for caution and diplomacy because he understood the military weakness of the Crusader states compared to the unified forces under Saladin. His opposition to the reckless policies of Reynald of Châtillon highlighted his strategic mindset though many viewed his caution as cowardice or even treasonous collaboration with the enemy.\n\n*Legacy and Final Years*\nThe disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 proved the validity of his warnings since the Crusader army was annihilated due to poor leadership and thirst induced mistakes. Raymond managed to escape the battlefield with a small contingent of knights while most others perished or were captured including King Guy. He died shortly after the battle possibly from illness or despair over the collapse of the kingdom he tried to protect through careful statecraft. Historians often debate his legacy since some contemporaries accused him of treachery while modern scholars recognize his realistic assessment of the geopolitical situation facing the Latin East. His life story illustrates the complex challenges faced by Crusader nobles who had to balance religious zeal with political survival in a hostile environment surrounded by powerful Muslim empires.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -5.710288e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0004940061}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0023330823}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.7898589}, {"token": " year", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -9.536748e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -1.7881409e-06}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -1.0967315e-05}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -7.748634e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.00025651167}, {"token": "